Q: JavaScript: Invert color on all elements of a page A: <p>I've seen multiple answers with bookmarklets, and using CSS filter on the entire <code>html</code> element looks pretty good, and it works both in Chrome and Firefox, so a wide browser support can be assumed. I identified some issues though:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Color change caused by inversion may cause confusion e.g. with color coding of correct as green and incorrect as red,</p>
</li>
<li><p>Some websites don't even use any style for <code>html</code> or <code>body</code> element, causing the <code>filter</code> not to apply on its <em>implicit</em> white background</p>
</li>
<li><p>Obviously, photos look terrible after inversion.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>I edited one of the solutions to deal with first two issues. Since whitespaces seem like a non-issue in Chrome and Firefox, I'll post it formatted:</p>
<pre><code>javascript:void(function(){
    invert = 90;
    contrast = 105;
    brightness = 105;
    style = document.getElementsByTagName('html')[0].style;
    if (style.backgroundColor == '') {
        style.backgroundColor = 'white';
    }
    if (style.filter == '') {
        style.filter = `invert(${invert}%) contrast(${contrast}%) brightness(${brightness}%) hue-rotate(180deg)`;
    } else {
        style.filter = '';
    }
}());
</code></pre>
<p>The solution uses the following to deal with mentioned issues:</p>
<ol>
<li><p><code>hue-rotate</code> by 180 degrees causes the colors to be closer to the original hue before inversion. This simple solution seems sufficient.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Applying explicit white background to a website that uses browser default allows for the background to be inverted correctly.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>I would love to give attribution to the author of original bookmarklet, but I am no longer aware who it is. It is useful that the configuration variables are in the front, making it easier to adjust it to your preferences. I prefer slightly lower contrast, it is easier on my eyes on a decent quality, high contrast monitor.</p>
<p>If you identify any further issues with this bookmarklet, feel free to comment, I'll gladly resolve more issues even though I'm not a JavaScript or CSS pro.</p>

Q: Does malloc failure imply ENOMEM? A: <blockquote>
<p>just checking for NULL is the correct design.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's not experience. I often write functions that are guaranteed to have <code>errno</code> set on error. On non-POSIX systems, this means you can't just check if <code>malloc</code> returns <code>NULL</code>; you need to take the additional step of setting <code>errno</code> when it does.</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>// `foo` returns `NULL` and sets errno on failure.
Type* foo( ... ) {
   Type *p = malloc( ... );
   if ( !p ) {
      errno = ENOMEM;
      return NULL;
   }

   int fd = open( ... );
   if ( fd == -1 ) {
      free( p );
      return NULL;
   }

   p-&gt;fd = fd;

   return p; 
}
</code></pre>

Q: plyr-react ref.current.plyr.on is not a function A: <p>I found the issue: when calling plyr.on(), the plyr object hasn't been initialized yet and is just a placeholder. You need to check if it's initialized first:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const isPlyrInitialized = (plyrInstance) =&gt; {
  // Check constructor name
  if (plyrInstance?.constructor?.name === '_Plyr') return true
  // Check for required methods
  if (typeof plyrInstance?.on === 'function') return true
  if (typeof plyrInstance?.play === 'function') return true
  return false
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// Wait for initialization before setting up listeners:

const plyrInstance = ref.current.plyr
if (!isPlyrInitialized(plyrInstance)) {
  const checkInterval = setInterval(() =&gt; {
    if (isPlyrInitialized(plyrInstance)) {
      clearInterval(checkInterval)
      // Now safe to use plyrInstance.on()
      plyrInstance.on('ended', () =&gt; {
        alert('Video completed!')
      })
    }
  }, 100)
}
</code></pre>
